### Step 1: Calculate the magnetic field due to coil P.

The magnetic field at the center of a circular coil is given by \(B = \frac{\mu_0 N i}{2r}\).
Given: Number of turns \(N = 100\), radius \(r = \pi \text{ cm} = \pi \times 10^{-2} \text{ m}\), current in coil P \(i_1 = 1\text{ A}\).
B_P = \frac{\mu_0 N i_1}{2r} = \frac{(4\pi \times 10^{-7}) \times 100 \times 1}{2 \times (\pi \times 10^{-2})}
B_P = \frac{4\pi \times 10^{-7} \times 100}{2\pi \times 10^{-2}} = 2 \times 10^{-3} \text{ T}

### Step 2: Calculate the magnetic field due to coil Q.

Given: Number of turns \(N = 100\), radius \(r = \pi \text{ cm} = \pi \times 10^{-2} \text{ m}\), current in coil Q \(i_2 = 2\text{ A}\).
B_Q = \frac{\mu_0 N i_2}{2r} = \frac{(4\pi \times 10^{-7}) \times 100 \times 2}{2 \times (\pi \times 10^{-2})}
